<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Wildcat Hub PowerQueries, coverage expansion set.

  Companion to wildcathub.named_queries.xml, which is NOT edited by this piece.
  A second file in queries_root is legal: named_queries.xml files live flat in
  queries_root and the query name attribute carries the namespace, not the path.

  ==========================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE COSTS TO SHIP, stated precisely because it is easy to get
  wrong in both directions.
  ==========================================================================
  Every column referenced below is ALREADY granted ViewOnly in plugin.xml.
  This file requests NO new data. Zero new <field> lines.

  It still cannot be called until the plugin is rebuilt, the version bumped,
  the zip re-uploaded and an admin disables and re-enables the plugin, because
  PowerQueries ship inside the plugin zip. So the deployment cost is one
  version bump; the APPROVAL cost is close to nothing, because the reviewer is
  being asked to accept four SELECT statements over data they already granted,
  not to widen access. Those are very different conversations and the request
  should say so.

  ==========================================================================
  SCHEMA RULES, inherited from the sibling file's hard won notes.
  ==========================================================================
    - Root element is <queries> with NO namespace and NO schemaLocation.
    - Order inside <query> is <description>, <args>, <columns>, <sql>.
    - EVERY bind variable used in the SQL must be declared in <args>. An
      undeclared :name installs cleanly with no validation error and then 404s
      on every call, forever. This is the single most expensive mistake in the
      file format.
    - The number of <column> entries must equal the number of columns the SQL
      returns, in the same order.
    - Each column attribute must name a real table and column that the access
      request grants; it drives permission mapping. Computed columns point at a
      granted core field by convention and let the alias carry the real name.
    - Every calculated column must be aliased.
    - SELECT only. No wildcard SELECT.

  ==========================================================================
  THE COURSES JOIN. Read this before copying the spine from the sibling file.
  ==========================================================================
  The sibling file's roster and grades queries both join COURSES like this:

      JOIN COURSES C ON C.COURSE_NUMBER = SEC.COURSE_NUMBER
                    AND C.SCHOOLID      = SEC.SCHOOLID

  That predicate is wrong on this instance and the first version of this file
  inherited it by copying. 130 of the 484 COURSES rows here are DISTRICT level
  and carry SCHOOLID 0. Westbrook sections point straight at them. `0 = 1817`
  is false, so an INNER JOIN on equality throws every one of those rows away
  and reports no error at all.

  Measured on live term 3601, the current school year:

      SECTIONS in the term ........................ 231
      surviving the equality predicate ............ 146
      live CC rows in the term .................... 5767
      surviving the equality predicate ............ 3805   (34.0 percent lost)
      distinct students touched by a lost row ..... 639 of 641

  Two consequences that are easy to miss:

    1. The project's own established figures, "3805 enrollments, 145 sections",
       are the POST DROP numbers. They are measurements of the defect, not of
       the school. Anything calibrated against them needs revisiting.
    2. Period token "9" exists on 340 enrollments and lives ENTIRELY inside the
       dropped set, so a period vocabulary query built on the equality
       predicate cannot see a whole period of the school day.

  This file therefore does two things the sibling file does not:

    - The predicate is `(C.SCHOOLID = SEC.SCHOOLID OR C.SCHOOLID = 0)`, which
      recovers the district level courses. It cannot fan rows out: no
      course_number in this instance exists at both the school and at 0, and no
      course_number appears twice at the same SCHOOLID. Both measured.
    - The join is a LEFT JOIN. Even with the OR, two Westbrook sections still
      resolve to nothing, because course_number 7002A ("RSP A") is defined only
      at school 1818. Those two sections carry 20 students. A course row that
      is missing is a reason to show a blank course name, never a reason to
      drop a child's attendance. The LEFT JOIN is what puts both queries in
      this file on the same basis as enrollment_window: EVERY live CC row.

  BASIS, stated once and repeated in each description so the three queries can
  never quietly disagree about how many sections a student has: every query in
  this file counts over every live CC row for the term, meaning DATELEFT null
  or in the future. On term 3601 that is 5767 rows. No query in this file drops
  a row because of a COURSES lookup.

  ==========================================================================
  ONE UNVERIFIED ASSUMPTION, called out rather than buried.
  ==========================================================================
  attendance_by_section joins ATTENDANCE to CC on ATTENDANCE.CCID. That column
  is granted and is referenced by nothing in the project today, so nobody has
  ever checked whether this instance populates it. If it is null, every count
  in attendance_by_section is a correct zero for the wrong reason.

  That is why attendance_join_health exists and why it is first in this file.
  Run it before anyone builds a screen on attendance_by_section. It answers the
  question in one call, returns one row, and carries no student data at all.
  attendance_by_section also returns attendance_rows_section_term so a caller
  can always tell "this student was never absent in this class" from "no
  attendance rows joined to this class at all". Those must never render the
  same way.
-->
<queries>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       0. ATTENDANCE JOIN HEALTH.

       One row. No student identifiers, no dates tied to a person, nothing
       that needs redacting. Purely: is the CCID join viable in this
       instance, and does this school year have any attendance in it yet?

       Read this before trusting query 1. The 26-27 school year began
       2026-08-12, so pass a prior :yearid to see a populated year and the
       current :yearid to see how far the current one has filled in.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.attendance_join_health" coreTable="attendance" flattened="true">
    <description>
      Diagnostic. Counts ATTENDANCE rows for one school and one year and
      reports how many carry a CCID and a PERIODID. Answers whether per
      section attendance is buildable at all before anyone builds on it.
      Returns exactly one row and no student level data.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
      <arg name="yearid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Attendance.Yearid for the school year being measured" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ID">attendance_rows</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.CCID">rows_with_ccid</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.PERIODID">rows_with_periodid</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ID">distinct_students</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ATT_MODE_CODE">distinct_mode_codes</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ATT_DATE">first_att_date</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ATT_DATE">last_att_date</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          COUNT(A.ID)                    AS attendance_rows,
          COUNT(A.CCID)                  AS rows_with_ccid,
          COUNT(A.PERIODID)              AS rows_with_periodid,
          COUNT(DISTINCT A.STUDENTID)    AS distinct_students,
          COUNT(DISTINCT A.ATT_MODE_CODE) AS distinct_mode_codes,
          MIN(A.ATT_DATE)                AS first_att_date,
          MAX(A.ATT_DATE)                AS last_att_date
        FROM ATTENDANCE A
        WHERE A.SCHOOLID = :schoolid
          AND A.YEARID   = :yearid
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       1. ATTENDANCE BY SECTION. Closes the accuracy ceiling that
       attendance_summary documents in its own comment: because this school
       records attendance per meeting, a student who cuts one period is
       indistinguishable from a student who missed the whole day.

       One row per student per section enrollment for one term. The section
       identity comes from the same CC / SECTIONS / COURSES spine the roster
       query already uses, so a caller can join this to psRoster on
       (student_id, section_id) with no new key.

       days_absent_section_term is COUNT(DISTINCT ATT_DATE) inside a single
       CC, not a row count, for the same reason the daily query counts
       distinct dates: a double period block writes two rows for one absence.

       attendance_rows_section_term is NOT decoration. Zero absences and zero
       rows mean different things and a dashboard must not draw them the same.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.attendance_by_section" coreTable="students" flattened="true">
    <description>
      Absences and tardies per student per section for one term, counted as
      distinct dates within each section enrollment. Includes the raw joined
      row count so a caller can distinguish a real zero from an empty join.
      LEFT JOINed throughout so a student with perfect attendance is returned
      as zero rather than dropped. BASIS: every live CC row for the term,
      DATELEFT null or in the future, 5767 rows on term 3601. The COURSES join
      is a LEFT JOIN with an OR on the district level SCHOOLID 0, so no
      enrollment is ever dropped for a course lookup. course_number comes from
      SECTIONS, which always has one; only course_name can be null.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
      <arg name="termid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Terms.ID for the term being read" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">student_id</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.STUDENT_NUMBER">student_number</column>
      <column column="SECTIONS.ID">section_id</column>
      <column column="SECTIONS.SECTION_NUMBER">section_number</column>
      <column column="CC.EXPRESSION">section_expression</column>
      <column column="SECTIONS.COURSE_NUMBER">course_number</column>
      <column column="COURSES.COURSE_NAME">course_name</column>
      <column column="TEACHERS.ID">teacher_id</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">days_absent_section_term</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">days_tardy_section_term</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">attendance_rows_section_term</column>
      <column column="ATTENDANCE.ATT_DATE">last_absence_date</column>
      <column column="TERMS.FIRSTDAY">term_first_day</column>
      <column column="TERMS.LASTDAY">term_last_day</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          S.ID                AS student_id,
          S.STUDENT_NUMBER    AS student_number,
          SEC.ID              AS section_id,
          SEC.SECTION_NUMBER  AS section_number,
          CC.EXPRESSION       AS section_expression,
          SEC.COURSE_NUMBER   AS course_number,
          C.COURSE_NAME       AS course_name,
          TCH.ID              AS teacher_id,
          COUNT(DISTINCT
            CASE WHEN AC.PRESENCE_STATUS_CD = 'Absent' THEN A.ATT_DATE END
          )                   AS days_absent_section_term,
          COUNT(DISTINCT
            CASE WHEN AC.ATT_CODE = 'T' THEN A.ATT_DATE END
          )                   AS days_tardy_section_term,
          COUNT(A.ID)         AS attendance_rows_section_term,
          MAX(
            CASE WHEN AC.PRESENCE_STATUS_CD = 'Absent' THEN A.ATT_DATE END
          )                   AS last_absence_date,
          TM.FIRSTDAY         AS term_first_day,
          TM.LASTDAY          AS term_last_day
        FROM STUDENTS S
        JOIN CC              ON CC.STUDENTID = S.ID
        JOIN SECTIONS SEC    ON SEC.ID = CC.SECTIONID
        LEFT JOIN COURSES C  ON C.COURSE_NUMBER = SEC.COURSE_NUMBER
                            AND (C.SCHOOLID = SEC.SCHOOLID OR C.SCHOOLID = 0)
        JOIN TERMS TM        ON TM.ID       = :termid
                            AND TM.SCHOOLID = :schoolid
        LEFT JOIN TEACHERS TCH ON TCH.ID = SEC.TEACHER
        LEFT JOIN ATTENDANCE A
          ON A.CCID    = CC.ID
         AND A.ATT_DATE BETWEEN TM.FIRSTDAY AND TM.LASTDAY
        LEFT JOIN ATTENDANCE_CODE AC
          ON AC.ID = A.ATTENDANCE_CODEID
        WHERE S.SCHOOLID      = :schoolid
          AND CC.TERMID       = :termid
          AND S.ENROLL_STATUS = 0
          AND (CC.DATELEFT IS NULL OR CC.DATELEFT >= TRUNC(SYSDATE))
        GROUP BY
          S.ID, S.STUDENT_NUMBER, SEC.ID, SEC.SECTION_NUMBER, CC.EXPRESSION,
          SEC.COURSE_NUMBER, C.COURSE_NAME, TCH.ID, TM.FIRSTDAY, TM.LASTDAY
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       2. ENROLLMENT WINDOW. Correct denominators.

       NAMED CAREFULLY. This is called enrollment_WINDOW and not
       enrollment_history because it is not history. STUDENTS holds the
       student's CURRENT enrollment only. A student's PRIOR spans at this
       school, which is what "transfer history" means, live in the
       REENROLLMENTS table. A live probe confirms REENROLLMENTS exists in
       this instance and every useful column on it answers 403, meaning it
       exists and is not granted. Anyone who needs transfer history needs
       that grant; this query cannot supply it and does not claim to.

       STUDENTS.ENTRYDATE, STUDENTS.EXITDATE and CC.DATEENROLLED have been
       granted since 1.0.0 and are selected by nothing. Without them a
       student who enrolled three weeks into a term is measured against the
       whole term. In a product that converts behaviour into currency, a
       denominator error is not cosmetic.

       WHAT IT IS WORTH TODAY, measured rather than assumed. A live read of
       all 644 active students found TWO distinct entry dates, because the
       probe ran the day before the school year opened and the whole school
       rolls over together. So today this query returns the same window for
       almost every student and changes no number on any screen. Across all
       1490 students on file, active and inactive, the same column holds 103
       distinct entry dates, so the variation is real, it just arrives with
       the students who arrive after day one. Ship it as a correct guard that
       is currently inert, and do not let anyone sell it as a fix for a
       visible problem.

       BASIS RECONCILIATION. section_count here and the row grain of
       attendance_by_section are now the same population by construction:
       every CC row for the term whose DATELEFT is null or in the future, with
       no COURSES lookup able to remove one. The DATELEFT clause on the CC
       LEFT JOIN below exists for that reason alone. Without it this query
       counts a dropped class and attendance_by_section does not, and two
       queries shipping in one file would report different section counts for
       the same student the first time anyone changes their timetable.

       calendar_days_enrolled_in_term is CALENDAR days, deliberately not
       school days. School days need the school calendar, which lives in
       CALENDAR_DAY, which this plugin does not grant. Naming it calendar
       days keeps a caller from dividing by it as though it were instruction
       days.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.enrollment_window" coreTable="students" flattened="true">
    <description>
      One row per active student for one term: school entry and exit dates,
      the clipped window during which the student was actually enrolled inside
      that term, and whether they entered late or left early. Gives every rate
      on the dashboard a denominator that matches the student rather than the
      calendar. This is the CURRENT enrollment window only; prior enrollment
      spans live in REENROLLMENTS, which this plugin does not grant. BASIS:
      section_count counts every live CC row for the term, with no COURSES
      lookup, which is the same basis attendance_by_section uses. The two
      cannot disagree about how many sections a student has.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
      <arg name="termid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Terms.ID for the term being read" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">student_id</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.STUDENT_NUMBER">student_number</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ENTRYDATE">school_entry_date</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.EXITDATE">school_exit_date</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ENROLL_STATUS">enroll_status</column>
      <column column="TERMS.FIRSTDAY">term_first_day</column>
      <column column="TERMS.LASTDAY">term_last_day</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ENTRYDATE">enrolled_from</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.EXITDATE">enrolled_to</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">calendar_days_enrolled_in_term</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">entered_after_term_start</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">exited_before_term_end</column>
      <column column="CC.DATEENROLLED">first_section_date_enrolled</column>
      <column column="STUDENTS.ID">section_count</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          S.ID              AS student_id,
          S.STUDENT_NUMBER  AS student_number,
          S.ENTRYDATE       AS school_entry_date,
          S.EXITDATE        AS school_exit_date,
          S.ENROLL_STATUS   AS enroll_status,
          TM.FIRSTDAY       AS term_first_day,
          TM.LASTDAY        AS term_last_day,
          GREATEST(NVL(S.ENTRYDATE, TM.FIRSTDAY), TM.FIRSTDAY) AS enrolled_from,
          LEAST(NVL(S.EXITDATE, TM.LASTDAY), TM.LASTDAY)       AS enrolled_to,
          (
            LEAST(NVL(S.EXITDATE, TM.LASTDAY), TM.LASTDAY)
            - GREATEST(NVL(S.ENTRYDATE, TM.FIRSTDAY), TM.FIRSTDAY)
            + 1
          )                 AS calendar_days_enrolled_in_term,
          CASE
            WHEN S.ENTRYDATE IS NOT NULL AND S.ENTRYDATE > TM.FIRSTDAY THEN 1
            ELSE 0
          END               AS entered_after_term_start,
          CASE
            WHEN S.EXITDATE IS NOT NULL AND S.EXITDATE < TM.LASTDAY THEN 1
            ELSE 0
          END               AS exited_before_term_end,
          MIN(CC.DATEENROLLED) AS first_section_date_enrolled,
          COUNT(CC.ID)         AS section_count
        FROM STUDENTS S
        JOIN TERMS TM
          ON TM.ID       = :termid
         AND TM.SCHOOLID = :schoolid
        LEFT JOIN CC
          ON CC.STUDENTID = S.ID
         AND CC.TERMID    = :termid
         AND (CC.DATELEFT IS NULL OR CC.DATELEFT >= TRUNC(SYSDATE))
        WHERE S.SCHOOLID      = :schoolid
          AND S.ENROLL_STATUS = 0
        GROUP BY
          S.ID, S.STUDENT_NUMBER, S.ENTRYDATE, S.EXITDATE, S.ENROLL_STATUS,
          TM.FIRSTDAY, TM.LASTDAY
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       3. PERIOD STRUCTURE.

       SECTIONMEETING was rejected as an invalid table when plugin 1.0.0 was
       validated, which left this project with no structured period source
       and no join path to PERIOD or CYCLE_DAY. The fallback the sibling file
       chose was to parse SECTIONS.EXPRESSION and CC.EXPRESSION.

       Meanwhile script.js hardcodes the bell schedule that ClawPass matches
       those expressions against: periods A1, P1 through P6, HPU and A2, with
       literal start and end times, maintained by hand. When a student scans
       out, detectCurrentPeriod() finds the period by wall clock time and
       then looks for a section whose period equals that token. A token the
       SIS uses and the app does not know produces no error. It produces a
       hall pass with the wrong teacher on it.

       This query returns the period vocabulary the SIS is actually using,
       with how many sections and how many students sit behind each token, so
       the hand maintained list can be diffed against reality instead of
       trusted. It is a configuration query, not a per student query: it
       returns no student identifiers at all.
       ================================================================== -->
  <query name="com.lapromisefund.wildcathub.period_structure" coreTable="students" flattened="true">
    <description>
      The distinct period expressions in live use for one school and term,
      with section, student and teacher counts behind each. Aggregate only,
      no student identifiers. Feeds a check of the ClawPass bell schedule
      against the SIS rather than a student facing screen. BASIS: every live CC
      row for the term, the same basis as the other two queries in this file.
      The COURSES join is a LEFT JOIN with an OR on the district level SCHOOLID
      0, without which period token 9 is invisible entirely and every other
      token under counts by 20 to 50 percent.
    </description>
    <args>
      <arg name="schoolid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Schools.School_Number for the school being read" />
      <arg name="termid" required="true" type="primitive" description="Terms.ID for the term being read" />
    </args>
    <columns>
      <column column="CC.EXPRESSION">section_expression</column>
      <column column="SECTIONS.ID">section_count</column>
      <column column="CC.STUDENTID">student_count</column>
      <column column="SECTIONS.TEACHER">teacher_count</column>
      <column column="COURSES.COURSE_NAME">example_course_name</column>
      <column column="TERMS.ID">term_id</column>
      <column column="TERMS.ABBREVIATION">term_abbreviation</column>
      <column column="CC.SCHOOLID">school_id</column>
    </columns>
    <sql>
      <![CDATA[
        SELECT
          NVL(CC.EXPRESSION, SEC.EXPRESSION) AS section_expression,
          COUNT(DISTINCT SEC.ID)             AS section_count,
          COUNT(DISTINCT CC.STUDENTID)       AS student_count,
          COUNT(DISTINCT SEC.TEACHER)        AS teacher_count,
          MIN(C.COURSE_NAME)                 AS example_course_name,
          T.ID                               AS term_id,
          T.ABBREVIATION                     AS term_abbreviation,
          CC.SCHOOLID                        AS school_id
        FROM CC
        JOIN SECTIONS SEC ON SEC.ID = CC.SECTIONID
        LEFT JOIN COURSES C ON C.COURSE_NUMBER = SEC.COURSE_NUMBER
                           AND (C.SCHOOLID = SEC.SCHOOLID OR C.SCHOOLID = 0)
        JOIN TERMS T      ON T.ID       = CC.TERMID
                         AND T.SCHOOLID = CC.SCHOOLID
        WHERE CC.SCHOOLID = :schoolid
          AND CC.TERMID   = :termid
          AND (CC.DATELEFT IS NULL OR CC.DATELEFT >= TRUNC(SYSDATE))
        GROUP BY
          NVL(CC.EXPRESSION, SEC.EXPRESSION), T.ID, T.ABBREVIATION, CC.SCHOOLID
      ]]>
    </sql>
  </query>

</queries>
